##
a struct is a datastructure that can hold multiple values of different types, it has no identity and is passed by value.
It is similar to a class, but it is typically used for small data structures that do not require inheritance or polymorphism.
it can be used in evaluations like a single value, and can be used in expressions like a single value. 
Code below the requirements section is a sample implementation of a struct and a class that uses it and should parse and compile as valid code
and run successfully.

**PYS Language Extension — Identity-Free Struct Type**

---

**1. Requirements**

**REQ-STRUCT-01**: PYS SHALL support a `struct` declaration as a sibling production to `class_decl`, producing identity-free value types.

**REQ-STRUCT-01.1**: `struct_decl` SHALL NOT support `inherits`/`super`/`sealed` — identity-free types have no polymorphic dispatch basis and no inheritance chain to seal.

**REQ-STRUCT-01.2**: `==` applied to two struct-typed operands SHALL compile to field-wise comparison, not the reference-equality path used for `class` instances.

**REQ-STRUCT-01.3**: `shared_decl` SHALL be statically rejected when `type_name` resolves to a `struct`.

**REQ-STRUCT-01.4**: Struct fields SHALL support the `fix` qualifier (assign-once at construction). A field without `fix` is mutable, permitted only if the enclosing struct binding itself is mutable (`var`, not `fix`).

**REQ-STRUCT-01.5**: Struct-typed fields SHALL reject `null_lit` as an initializer or assigned value, enforced via static analysis.

**REQ-STRUCT-01.6**: Struct instances SHALL NOT use identity-based hashing; hash derivation SHALL be field-state-based, consistent with REQ-STRUCT-01.2.

**REQ-STRUCT-01.7**: When a struct-typed binding is declared with `fix`, the entire instance SHALL be immutable — including fields declared without a per-field `fix` qualifier. Per-field `fix` remains meaningful only for `var`-bound instances; on a `fix`-bound instance, per-field mutability is moot, since no field can be reassigned regardless of its own qualifier.

REQ-STRUCT-01.8: A struct declared with a leading fix qualifier SHALL be immutable for all instances of that type, irrespective of per-field fix qualifiers or binding form (var/fix). All fields SHALL be treated as assign-once at construction.

REQ-STRUCT-01.9: Structs declared without a leading fix qualifier SHALL default to mutable, consistent with prevailing industry practice (C# structs, Go structs, Swift's let/var-per-binding model) — per-field or per-binding fix remains the opt-in mechanism for partial or instance-level immutability.

REQ-STRUCT-01.10: A struct type SHALL expose an implicit canonical constructor derived from its struct_field_decl list, in declaration order. Construction SHALL be expressible via constructor_call (positional or named arg_list, per existing grammar) without a new keyword and without a user-defined constructor_decl.

REQ-STRUCT-01.11: Positional arguments to a struct's canonical constructor SHALL map to fields in declaration order; named arguments SHALL map by field identifier and MAY appear in any order or be partially omitted only if the corresponding field has a default expression in struct_field_decl.
---

**2. EBNF**

Insert as a new section under Classes, before Interfaces:

```ebnf
(* ------------------------- Structs ------------------------- *)

struct_decl      = [ top_visibility ] , [ "fix" ] , "struct" , identifier ,
                   [ type_params ] , struct_body ;

(* No "sealed", no "inherits"/"super" — structs are identity-free and
   participate in no inheritance chain. No "implements" — structs carry
   no behavior, so interface conformance does not apply.
   top_visibility controls who may import the type; fields are always public. *)

struct_body      = "{" , { struct_field_decl } , "}" ;

struct_field_decl = [ "fix" ] , type_name , identifier ,
                    [ "=" , expression ] ;
```

Amend `declaration`:

```ebnf
declaration      = var_decl
                 | const_decl
                 | fix_decl
                 | shared_decl
                 | function_decl
                 | class_decl
                 | struct_decl
                 | interface_decl ;
```

Amend `fix_decl` to allow named types (structs), not only primitives:

```ebnf
fix_decl         = [ top_visibility ] , "fix" , ( primitive_type | named_type ) ,
                   identifier , "=" , expression ;
```

Amend Appendix B (Keywords) — add `struct`:

```
if else unless loop while for repeat times then do elif
function func return class struct interface implements inherits super sealed
import from all var const fix print pass break continue
this and or not
public private protected global package module
int float char string bool list dict tuple set
true false null
tasks task await shared
```

---

**3. Static Analysis Constraints (not expressible in EBNF)**

| # | Constraint | Enforcement point |
|---|---|---|
| SA-1 | Reject `shared <StructType> ...` | Type resolution pass on `shared_decl` |
| SA-2 | Reject `null_lit` as initializer/assignment target for struct-typed field | Type-check pass on `struct_field_decl` and `assignment` |
| SA-3 | `==` on struct operands routes to field-wise comparison, not reference equality | Codegen/emit layer |
| SA-4 | Hash derivation consistent with `==` (field-based, not identity-based) | Codegen/emit layer |
| SA-5 | Parser SHALL emit a specific error (not generic syntax error) if `inherits`/`super`/`sealed`/`implements` follows `struct` | Parser |
| SA-6 | Reject any `assignment` targeting a field of a `fix`-bound struct instance, regardless of the field's own `fix` qualifier | Type-check pass on `assignment` against binding mutability |
| SA-7 | If struct_decl carries fix, reject assignment to any field of any instance of that type, regardless of per-field qualifier or binding form
| SA-8 | Reject new preceding a struct-typed constructor_call	Parser/type-check pass
| SA-9 | Positional arg_list length SHALL match struct field count unless trailing fields have defaults	Type-check pass on constructor_call against struct_field_decl
| SA-10 | Named arg_list identifiers SHALL each resolve to a declared field of the target struct	Type-c
---

**4. Mutability Matrix**

| Struct declaration | Binding form | Field-level qualifier | Result |
|---|---|---|---|
| `struct Damage { ... }` | `Damage d = Damage(...)` / `var d = Damage(...)` | no `fix` | field reassignable |
| `struct Damage { ... }` | `Damage d = Damage(...)` / `var d = Damage(...)` | `fix` | field frozen, binding itself still rebindable |
| `struct Damage { ... }` | `fix Damage d = Damage(...)` | irrelevant | entire instance frozen, binding not rebindable |
| `fix struct Damage { ... }` | `Damage d = Damage(...)` / `var d = Damage(...)` | irrelevant | all fields frozen (type-level), binding itself still rebindable |
| `fix struct Damage { ... }` | `fix Damage d = Damage(...)` | irrelevant | all fields frozen (type-level), binding also not rebindable |

**Similarities with a Python dict**

- Both are pure data containers without meaningful identity in usage context — comparison happens on value, not reference (`dict.__eq__` compares key/value pairs; struct `==` compares fields).
- Construction is value-oriented like filling a dict, but PYS uses the canonical constructor: `Damage(20, "physical")` / `Damage(amount=20, type="physical")` ↔ `{"amount": 20, "type": "physical"}` (no brace field literals for structs).
- Mutable by default (dict values overwritable; struct fields without `fix` overwritable).
- No behavior/methods — REQ-STRUCT-01 restricts `struct_member` to fields only, exactly like a dict has no built-in per-instance behavior.

**Differences**

| Aspect | `struct` (PYS) | Python `dict` |
|---|---|---|
| Typing | Static, nominal — `Damage` is a fixed schema with declared types per field | Dynamic, structural — arbitrary keys, no schema, heterogeneous value types allowed |
| Schema | Fixed at declaration; no runtime add/remove of fields | Open; keys freely added/removed at runtime |
| Nominal type identity | Two structs with identical fields but different declared type are *not* interchangeable (compiler tracks the distinction) | Two dicts with identical key/value pairs are unconditionally equal — no "type" concept |
| Per-field immutability | `fix` per field possible (REQ-STRUCT-01.4) | No native per-key immutability; requires a wrapper (e.g. `MappingProxyType`) |
| Access control | Fields always public; `top_visibility` on the struct controls import of the type | No access control; all keys visible at runtime |
| Null values | Fields reject `null_lit` (REQ-STRUCT-01.5) | Values may freely be `None` |
| Hashability | If all fields are `fix`, potentially hashable (usable as set member/dict key) | Unhashable due to mutability — a dict cannot itself be a dict key |
| Memory representation | Fixed field slots (comparable to `dataclass`/`namedtuple`), predictable layout | Hashmap-based, per-instance overhead for key storage |
| Ordering | Field order fixed by declaration | Insertion order guaranteed (Python 3.7+), but dynamically alterable |

In short: a struct in this design is the schema-fixed, nominally-typed counterpart to a dict — where the dict offers structural flexibility without type guarantees, the struct offers type guarantees without flexibility.
/#

struct Damage {
    int amount
    string type
}

# fix struct is immutable for all instances of that type, irrespective of per-field fix qualifiers or binding form (var/fix). All fields SHALL be treated as assign-once at construction.
fix struct DamageFix {
    int amount
    string type
}

# fix struct is immutable for all instances of that type, irrespective of per-field fix qualifiers or binding form (var/fix). All fields SHALL be treated as assign-once at construction.
struct DamageFix2 {
    fix int amount 
    fix string type
}

#
struct DamageFix3 {
    int amount 
    fix string type
}

class Unit {
    private string name
    private int health
    private int mana

    public constructor(string name, int health, int mana) {
        this.name = name
        this.health = health
        this.mana = mana
    }

    public takeDamage(Damage damage) {
        this.health -= damage.amount
        if (this.health < 0) {
            this.health = 0
        }
    }

    public doDamage(Unit target, Damage damage) {
        target.takeDamage(damage)
    }
}

Unit u1 = Unit("Warrior", 100, 50)
Damage d1 = Damage(20, "physical")
Damage d2 = Damage(amount = 20, type = "physical")  # named arguments are optional, this should be implemented in the core langue for methods as well   
fix Damage d3 = Damage(21, "physical")
var d4 = Damage(20, "electric")

print(d1 == d2) # should print true, as the field values are equal
print(d1 == d3) # should print false, as the field values are not equal  
print(d4.type) # should print "electric"

d1.amount = 21 # should be allowed, as d1 is mutable

print(d1 == d3) # should print true, as the field values are now equal 

d3.amount = 22 # should be rejected, as d3 is fix-bound and immutable